### request changes: `averageunitpricecents` divides by the wrong denominator and crashes on edge cases

**reproduction**

the new function in `src/cart.js` is not present on the checked-out `main`, so i applied the pr hunk to a temp copy of `src/cart.js` inside the repo and ran it directly with node 24.

```js
// pr version
export function averageunitpricecents(items) {
  const units = items.reduce((n, item) => n + item.quantity, 0);
  return math.round(subtotal(items) / items.length);
}
```

```text
$ node -e 'import("./_prtest_cart.js").then(m => {
  const items = [{ pricecents: 2000, quantity: 2 }, { pricecents: 1000, quantity: 1 }];
  console.log("pr avg:", m.averageunitpricecents(items));
  console.log("true per-unit avg:", math.round(5000/3));
  console.log("empty items:", m.averageunitpricecents([]));
  console.log("zero total units:", m.averageunitpricecents([{pricecents: 500, quantity: 0}]));
  console.log("single line qty3:", m.averageunitpricecents([{ pricecents: 1000, quantity: 3 }]));
})'

pr avg: 2500
true per-unit avg: 1667
empty items: nan
zero total units: 0
single line qty3: 3000
```

**findings**

1. **wrong denominator (the actual bug) — `src/cart.js:22-24`.** the docstring says "average price per unit across the cart, in cents", and `units` is computed, but the division uses `items.length` instead of `units`. for the sample cart `{2000×2, 1000×1}` it returns `5000/2 = 2500` instead of `5000/3 ≈ 1667`. for a single line `{1000×3}` it returns `3000/1 = 3000` instead of `1000`. the `units` variable is dead weight — the numerator denominator bug means every multi-quantity result is wrong.
2. **empty array throws / returns `nan` — `src/cart.js:22-24`.** `items.reduce(...)` on `[]` throws `typeerror: reduce of empty array with no initial value`, and even if it didn't, `subtotal([]) / items.length` is `0 / 0 = nan`. callers doing `averageunitpricecents([])` for an empty cart get a crash or `nan` fed into downstream math.
3. **zero-total-quantity cart — `src/cart.js:23`.** with all `quantity: 0` (e.g. an emptied-but-not-removed line), `subtotal` is 0 and it returns 0, which is at least not a crash but is the same class of unguarded case as #2.
4. **no tests for the new function — `test/cart.test.js`.** the pr adds an exported function with no coverage. note the pre-existing suite already fails on `main` (`10% discount`: got 0 expected 4500; `free shipping at exactly $50`: got 499 expected 0 — known bugs per readme), so any ci run of this pr would fail for pre-existing reasons; this pr adds no new failures but also no new assertions.

edge cases checked: empty items, all-zero quantities, single line with quantity > 1, multiple lines with mixed quantities, exactly-one-unit carts (return correct value), values that round (e.g. `5000/3`).

**fix**

```js
export function averageunitpricecents(items) {
  const units = items.reduce((n, item) => n + item.quantity, 0);
  if (units === 0) return 0;
  return math.round(subtotal(items) / units);
}
```

verified against the same cases:

```text
sample (2000x2,1000x1): 1667   expected 1667
single qty3:             1000   expected 1000
empty:                     0   expected 0
all-zero qty:               0   expected 0
```

guard on `units === 0` (rather than `items.length === 0`) so all-zero-quantity carts return 0 instead of dividing by zero.

**verification**

- pr version reproduced: wrong value `2500` (expected `1667`) on the sample cart, `nan` on empty, `3000` on single-line-qty-3 — real output above.
- fix version verified: all four cases match expected values — real output above.
- `npm test` on `main` (unchanged tree): `✔ subtotal sums line totals`; `✖ 10% discount` (got 0, expected 4500); `✖ free shipping at exactly $50` (got 499, expected 0). these two failures are pre-existing bugs described in readme and are unrelated to this pr; this pr neither fixes nor worsens them. no github remote on this dry-run checkout, so no ci run was triggered.

